# The same gate, a second row, for the box check's hoist. compile_ir's own
# mutation stops the vein being read at all; this one leaves the vein alone
# and asks whether it can still see one pass doing work it can prove is
# pointless. The two answer different questions about the same number.
box_check_hoist = row
  "a whole-program check that asks a question the program already answered"
  false
  "sh scripts/gates/compile_instructions.sh"
  "cost goldens (deterministic ratchet, no clocks)"
  (breaking "the_box_check_asks_when_nothing_answers_a_box")
  release

# A third row on the same gate, for the qualified-name join. compile_ir's
# mutation stops the vein being read; box_check_hoist asks whether it can see
# a pass doing provably pointless work; this one asks whether it can see the
# cost of a shape -- the same strings, built the expensive way. All three
# answer different questions about the same number.
qualified_name_join = row
  "a name the loader mints, built through the formatting machinery"
  false
  "sh scripts/gates/compile_instructions.sh"
  "cost goldens (deterministic ratchet, no clocks)"
  (breaking "the_qualified_name_goes_back_through_format")
  release

# The same shape for the ENTRY path, which the row above cannot reach: it
# checks a directory, and a directory takes compile_module_inner. The mutation
# asks the entry's whole-program check twice, and the two rows separate
# cleanly on it -- entry_instructions 165,183,406 -> 190,382,616 (+15.25%) in
# the box, compile_instructions byte-identical at 49,162,592. A vein whose
# defects another vein already catches would not be worth its callgrind run.
entry_ir = row
  "work on the entry path, where the compile row measures a module"
  false
  "sh scripts/gates/entry_instructions.sh"
  "cost goldens (deterministic ratchet, no clocks)"
  (breaking "the_entry_program_is_checked_twice")
  release

# And the LIBRARY path, which neither row above reaches. `kanso check` routes a
# single file by its content: bare statements make it an entry and take
# compile_parsed_entry, definitions alone make it a library and take
# compile_library, and the two run different passes in a different order. The
# mutation asks the library program's whole-program check twice --
# library_instructions 165,589,540 -> 190,698,277 (+15.16%) in the box, with the
# entry and module rows untouched, so the three separate cleanly.
library_ir = row
  "work on the library path, which the entry and module rows both walk past"
  false
  "sh scripts/gates/library_instructions.sh"
  "cost goldens (deterministic ratchet, no clocks)"
  (breaking "the_library_program_is_checked_twice")
  release

